# NestJS coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules, and `ts-js-node`'s
own TypeScript conventions, to the official NestJS idiom: decorator-driven
modules, providers, and dependency injection. Applies only to `*.ts` files
in a NestJS project.

## Naming and file layout

- One artifact per file, named after its role (`<name>.controller.ts`,
  `.service.ts`, `.module.ts`, `.dto.ts`, `.guard.ts`, `.interceptor.ts`,
  `.filter.ts`, `.pipe.ts`), the Nest CLI generator convention; no guard or
  interceptor folded into a controller file.
- Class names end in the file-role suffix (`UsersController`, `UsersService`,
  `UsersModule`).
- Injection tokens for non-class providers (`useValue`/`useFactory`) are
  `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` string constants or `Symbol()`s exported from a single
  `tokens.ts`/`constants.ts`, not inline string literals.

## Decorators and typing

- Every constructor-injected dependency is typed explicitly; never widen it
  to `any` to sidestep a circular-import error, fix the cycle (see
  `rules/patterns.mdc`).
- Every controller method's return type is explicit or inferred from a typed
  service call, never `Promise<any>`.
- DTOs are typed classes with `class-validator`/`class-transformer`
  decorators, never plain `interface`s for request bodies (see
  `rules/security.mdc` and the repo's `nestjs-dto.mdc` rule, which owns the
  DTO decorator checklist).
- `@Injectable()` on every class registered as a provider, even one with no
  constructor dependencies (see `skills/nestjs-build-fix/SKILL.md`).

## Imports and module boundaries

- Import providers only from the module that exports them; import the
  feature module, or add the provider to its `exports`, rather than reaching
  into another feature's `*.service.ts`.
- Keep an `@Module()` within one screen; split the feature into sub-modules
  past that.
- Barrel files export only what other modules are meant to import.

## Errors

- Throw a built-in NestJS exception (`NotFoundException`,
  `BadRequestException`, `ConflictException`) or a project subclass of
  `HttpException`, never a bare `throw new Error(...)` where an HTTP response
  is expected.
- Catch in a service only to add context (wrap in a domain-specific
  exception) or to handle it, not to log and rethrow unchanged.
